--TEST--
A closure parameter declaring an enum is checked as that enum's backing integer, so an enum, its backing type and any other enum with the same backing are indistinguishable while the two backings are told apart — `rule:types/conversion`, `rule:types/conversion` and `rule:types/arithmetic`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The depth claim is *agreement*: one question asked of four subjects that
// share a rule, asserting that they agree rather than what each answered.
// `nvs_ir::lower::param_tag_nibble` folds `Ty::Enum(EnumRepr::Int)` onto
// `Ty::Int`'s nibble and `Ty::Enum(EnumRepr::Uint)` onto `Ty::Uint`'s, because
// `rule:enums/closed-integer-type` makes an enum case a named integer that travels as its backing
// integer, tag included. So the check has no way to tell an enum from that
// integer, or from another enum backed the same way — and it does still tell
// `int` from `uint`, which is what keeps this a line rather than a hole: the
// nibble is exact about the one thing it names.
enum Mode: int {
    Read = 1,
    Write = 2,
}
// A second `int`-backed enum sharing nothing with `Mode` but its backing.
enum Level: int {
    Low = 1,
    High = 2,
}
// The same set of cases over the other backing, which is the far side of the
// bound: `Weight` and `Mode` are told apart, `Weight` and `uint` are not.
enum Weight: uint {
    Light = 1,
    Heavy = 2,
}

// Nine declarations, asked of every subject in the same order. A closure
// cannot be *called* through the variable holding it, but handing one to a
// `Core` member's `callable` option is the runtime's own call and does work,
// which is what lets the same nine be reused across four subjects rather than
// written out four times.
array<callable> $decls = [
    fn (Mode $v, string $k) => true,
    fn (Level $v, string $k) => true,
    fn (Weight $v, string $k) => true,
    fn (int $v, string $k) => true,
    fn (uint $v, string $k) => true,
    fn (string $v, string $k) => true,
    fn (bool $v, string $k) => true,
    // `rule:types/conversion`'s one implicit widening reaches an enum for the same reason
    // the check does: the argument carries an `int` tag, so a `float`
    // parameter widens it rather than refusing.
    fn (float $v, string $k) => true,
    // `rule:types/conversion`'s one unchecked position, the control that accepts every
    // subject.
    fn (mixed $v, string $k) => true,
];

array<Mode> $modes = [Mode::Read, Mode::Write];
array<int> $ints = [7, 8];
array<Weight> $weights = [Weight::Light, Weight::Heavy];
// The elements are converted rather than written as bare literals: an integer
// literal in an array-literal element position keeps the `int` representation
// whatever the array's declared element type says, so `[7, 8]` here would be
// asking the other backing's question.
array<uint> $uints = [7 as uint, 8 as uint];

// `y` is "the member ran", `n` is "the call threw `LogicError`". The claim is
// that lines 1 and 2 are the same line and lines 3 and 4 are the same line —
// an enum agreeing with its backing integer, twice, over two backings that
// disagree with each other.
// An option bag's `{by:}` rather than a whole parameter: every `Core\Arr`
// member whose callback is a *parameter* now declares its signature
// (`rule:types/callable-signature`), which a bare `callable` variable does not
// satisfy, and a bag's field is what is still handed to the runtime unchecked.
// `unique` calls `by` once per element and compares what it answered, so what
// each line records is still whether the *call* was made or refused.
var $a = "";
foreach ($decls as callable $d) {
    try { Core\Arr::unique($modes, {by: $d}); $a = $a . "y"; }
    catch (LogicError $e) { $a = $a . "n"; }
}
var $b = "";
foreach ($decls as callable $d) {
    try { Core\Arr::unique($ints, {by: $d}); $b = $b . "y"; }
    catch (LogicError $e) { $b = $b . "n"; }
}
var $c = "";
foreach ($decls as callable $d) {
    try { Core\Arr::unique($weights, {by: $d}); $c = $c . "y"; }
    catch (LogicError $e) { $c = $c . "n"; }
}
var $u = "";
foreach ($decls as callable $d) {
    try { Core\Arr::unique($uints, {by: $d}); $u = $u . "y"; }
    catch (LogicError $e) { $u = $u . "n"; }
}
echo $a, "\n", $b, "\n", $c, "\n", $u, "\n";

// And the argument arrives as itself under either declaration, unconverted and
// untested: `7` reaches a `Mode` parameter although `Mode` has no case backed
// by it, and `Mode::Read` reaches a `Level` parameter as the `1` it is. The
// closed-set membership `as Mode` tests (`rule:types/enum-case-type`) belongs to the
// conversion, and a `callable`'s parameter is not one. `countBy` carries the
// callback's answer out here for the reason `unique` does above — its `{by:}`
// is a bare `callable`, and its keys are what that callback answered — where
// `mapKeys`' written signature would refuse a `Mode` parameter over an
// `array<int>` before the run: the same disagreement, answered earlier, which
// is `rule:types/callable-signature`'s point rather than a contradiction of
// this one.
echo Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::keys(Core\Arr::countBy($ints, {by: fn (Mode $v, string $k) => ($v as int) as string})), ","), "\n";
echo Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::keys(Core\Arr::countBy($modes, {by: fn (Level $v, string $k) => ($v as int) as string})), ","), "\n";
--EXPECT--
yynynnnyy
yynynnnyy
nnynynnyy
nnynynnyy
7,8
1,2
